course: english
lesson: 9
title: Suffixes -ous, -ly, -ful, -less, -ness, -ment
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A suffix is a few letters added to the end of a word that change its job or meaning. «-ful» means full of (hopeful), «-less» means without (useless), «-ness» makes a feeling or state into a thing (kindness), «-ment» makes a thing from a verb (enjoyment), «-ly» often tells how (slowly), and «-ous» makes describing words meaning full of (famous, dangerous).
explain: Each suffix has its own spelling, and it stays the same. «-ful» has only one l, though the word «full» has two: careful, beautiful. «-ous» never ends in e: famous, not «famouse», even though «house» has one. When a word ends in -our, the u usually drops before -ous: humour, humorous; glamour, glamorous.
explain: The root word can change at the join. Consonant + y turns into i: happy, happily, happiness; easy, easily. Words ending in -ic add -ally: basic, basically; dramatic, dramatically (the famous exception is «publicly»). Endings that start with a consonant (-less, -ful, -ness, -ment) usually leave the root alone: use, useless; move, movement. But «argue» drops its e in «argument»: «I lost an e in an argument.»
explain: «-able» and «-ible» both mean «can be». «-able» is more common and usually follows a whole word you can recognise (enjoyable, readable); «-ible» often follows a part that is not a word by itself (horrible, visible). Before -able the e of the root usually drops (adore, adorable), but it stays after a soft c or g to keep them soft: notice, noticeable; change, changeable.
explain: Chanting lists can help you remember, as Grandma did, but understanding root plus suffix lets you spell words you have never met. Split «hopelessness» and it is just hope + less + ness.
fact: A suffix is added to the end of a word and changes its meaning or job: -ful (full of), -less (without), -ness (a state), -ment (a thing), -ly (how), -ous (full of).
fact: The suffix -ous never ends in e: famous, nervous, enormous.
fact: Before -ous, -our usually loses its u: humour, humorous; glamour, glamorous.
fact: The suffix -ful has one l: careful, hopeful, beautiful.
fact: Consonant + y changes to i before -ly and -ness: happily, happiness; easily, laziness.
fact: Words ending in -ic add -ally: basically, dramatically; «publicly» is the exception.
fact: Endings that start with a consonant (-less, -ful, -ness, -ment) usually do not change the root: useless, careless, enjoyment, movement.
fact: «Argue» drops its e in «argument» and «arguing».
fact: Before -able, a final e usually drops (adorable) but stays after soft c or g (noticeable, changeable).
fact: -able usually follows a whole word (enjoyable); -ible often follows a part that is not a word on its own (horrible, visible).
fact: Splitting a word into root and suffixes helps you spell words you have never seen.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «tamping» means very angry in South Wales; «nesh» means feeling the cold easily in the North of England.
exercise: Anya writes «famouse». What is right? | famous | famos | fameous | famouss
exercise: Tim writes «humourous». What does his teacher write? | humorous | humourus | humerous | humourous
exercise: Molly writes «carefull». Why is it wrong? | The suffix -ful has only one l | The word needs two r's: «carreful» | It should end in -fully, «carefully», to be a word | It needs an e at the end: «carefulle»
exercise: Rhys in Swansea is «tamping». How does he feel? | Very angry | Very cold | Very happy | Very tired
exercise: Rhys wrote «happyly». What is right? | happily | happilly | happyli | hapily
exercise: Ivy writes «basicly». What is right? | basically | basicaly | basiclly | basicully
exercise: Which -ic word just adds -ly? | publicly | basically | dramatically | magically
exercise: Oscar writes «arguement». What is right? | argument | argumint | arguemant | argumentt
exercise: Kelsey is «nesh». What does that mean? | She feels the cold easily | She is very brave | She is useless at spelling | She is in a bad mood
exercise: What does -less mean in «useless»? | Without | Full of | Very | Again
exercise: Toby writes «happyness». What is right? | happiness | happyniss | hapiness | happynes
exercise: Grandma learned suffixes by chanting. What else helps? | Splitting words into root and suffix | Chanting louder and for longer | Only ever learning words by shape | Never writing the words down
exercise: Why does «noticeable» keep the e? | To keep the c soft, like /s/ | Because all -able words keep the e | The e is there by mistake | To make the word longer
exercise: Which word drops its e before -able? | adore | change | notice | manage
exercise: Ella mixes up -able and -ible. Which usually follows a whole word? | -able | -ible | both, always | neither
exercise: Which word means «full of hope»? | hopeful | hopeless | hopefull | hopely
test: «I'm tamping, I wrote happyly and she marked it wrong». What should Rhys remember? | Consonant + y changes to i: happily | Just add -ly to every word, whatever it ends in | Double the y: happyyly | Drop the y: happly
test: «careful's got full in it, so two l's, obvs». What's the slip? | The suffix -ful has one l, unlike the word «full» | Nothing, «carefull» is right | It's «carful» with no e | It's «caerful» with ae, like in old books
test: «basicly, I'm right, innit». What is the spelling of his first word? | basically | basicly | basicaly | basiklly
test: What would happen if -ous ended in e, like «house»? | «Famous» would be spelled «famouse», which it isn't | Nothing would change in any word | «House» would lose its e | All -ous words would rhyme with «house»
test: «argue, so arguement, same as enjoyment keeps nothing». Put him right. | «Argument» is an exception: argue drops its e | «Arguement» is right because roots never change | It should be «argumint» | It should be «argueing» as a noun
test: «I'm nesh and useless today, can't even spell useles». What's right? | useless: use + less, nothing changes | useles: one s is enough at the end of a word | usless: drop the e of use | uselless: double the l
test: In a cartoon, a teacher writes «humourous» on the board. What should it be? | humorous: the u of humour drops | humourous: keep the root exactly | humerous: like «humerus» the bone | humorus: drop the o too
test: «adore → adoreable, notice → noticable, it's just opposite day». What is right? | adorable and noticeable | adoreable and noticable | adorable and noticable | adoreable and noticeable
test: «visible is v-i-s + ible, so readable should be readible». Is that right? | No: «read» is a whole word, so -able | Yes, -ible always follows a verb | Yes, all «can be» words use -ible | No, it should be «readabel», with the e and l swapped
test: ★★★ Split «carelessness» into its parts. | care + less + ness | carel + ess + ness | care + lessness | ca + reless + ness
test: ★★★ How many of these are spelled correctly: happiness, basically, carefull, humorous, argument? | 4 | 5 | 3 | 2
test: ★★★ Which word follows a rule that «publicly» breaks? | tragically | quickly | hopefully | happily, because of the y
exam: Ascertain the correct spelling of the word formed by the addition of the suffix -ness to the adjective «happy». | happiness | happyness | happieness | hapiness
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the final e is retained in the word «changeable». | It preserves the soft pronunciation of the letter g | It indicates that the word is of French origin | It is retained in every word ending in -able | It distinguishes the adjective from a verb of the same form
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the suffix -ful. | It is spelled with a single l, notwithstanding the spelling of the word «full» | It is spelled with two l's in all circumstances, in conformity with the spelling of the word «full» | It is spelled -full in British English and -ful in American English | It is spelled with a single l only in words of one syllable
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the suffix -less in the word «useless»? | It signifies «without» and is appended without alteration of the root | It signifies «full of» and requires the omission of the final e | It signifies «again» and requires the doubling of the s | It signifies «before» and is placed at the beginning of the word
